/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  column15_klemola_ideal_trays -- the CONTROL ARM of the Klemola-Ilme anchor.

  This is column14_klemola_c4_splitter with ONE line changed:

      murphreeEfficiency 1.191;      ->      murphreeEfficiency 1.0;

  Everything else -- the 74 trays, the feed on tray 37, the feed state, the
  pressure, the distillate rate, the reflux ratio, the thermo dict, the three
  0/ files, the convergence controls -- is byte-identical to that case, and if
  the two ever drift apart this case stops being a control.  Read
  column14's flowsheetDict for the provenance of every number; it is the
  primary document and none of it is repeated here.

      Klemola, K. T.; Ilme, J. K.  Distillation Efficiencies of an
      Industrial-Scale i-Butane/n-Butane Fractionator.
      Ind. Eng. Chem. Res. 1996, 35 (12), 4579-4586.

  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  WHY THIS IS A SEPARATE CASE AND NOT A SECOND ARM INSIDE column14
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

  A Choupo case is ONE pass: one converged/ state, one KPI set, one golden.
  Two efficiencies could have been swept in a single case through
  system/outerDict, and that was rejected: a sweep publishes the DRIVER's
  KPI table, so the two arms' products would live in a sweep artefact instead
  of in two real converged/ directories, and the anchor -- which is an
  ENDPOINT anchor, a comparison of product compositions -- would have nowhere
  natural to sit.  Two cases give two converged states, two goldens, and two
  independently sealable units of evidence, at the cost of the duplication
  named above.

  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  WHAT THE GAP BETWEEN THE ARMS MEANS -- and what it does NOT
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

  E_MV = 1 makes every one of the 74 trays an EQUILIBRIUM stage: the vapour
  leaving it is in equilibrium with the liquid leaving it.  No real tray does
  that, and this plant's trays do BETTER than that -- the paper back-calculates
  119.1 %, because on a 2.9 m cross-flow tray the liquid is not well mixed, so
  the vapour has been in contact with liquid richer than the outlet.

  The difference between this case's products and column14's is therefore the
  answer to a question a student can otherwise only be told: how much of an
  industrial separation is carried by the tray efficiency, as against the tray
  COUNT and the reflux?  Run both and subtract.  The paper's own version of the
  same arithmetic is that 74 real trays behave as 88 ideal ones.

  WHAT IT IS NOT.  This case is not an anchor and must never be compared with
  the Table-2 measurements as though it were one: it deliberately declares an
  efficiency the plant does not have.  Its role is to be the OTHER END of a
  one-parameter difference.  If it happened to sit closer to the measured
  products than column14 does, that would be a finding about the model, not a
  reason to prefer E_MV = 1 -- 119.1 % is the measurement.

  Note also that E_MV = 1 is the engine's DEFAULT, and the key is written out
  in full below anyway.  An absent key would mean the same thing to the solver
  and something quite different to a reader: it would look like an oversight in
  a case whose entire subject is the efficiency.

  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  WHAT THE TWO ARMS ACTUALLY GAVE
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

      quantity                        E_MV 1.191    E_MV 1.0    measured
      -------------------------------------------------------------------
      distillate i-butane, wt %          93.904      93.831       93.5
      distillate n-butane, wt %           0.0082      0.0755       0.2
      bottoms    i-butane, wt %           0.411       0.444        0.3
      bottoms    n-butane, wt %          97.979      97.949       98.1
      top temperature, K                319.767<!--golden: @steady/distillation/column14_klemola_c4_splitter kpi splitter T_top-->     319.646<!--golden: kpi splitter T_top-->       --
      reboiler temperature, K           335.085<!--golden: @steady/distillation/column14_klemola_c4_splitter kpi splitter T_bottom-->     335.081<!--golden: kpi splitter T_bottom-->       --
      reboiler duty, MW                   9.762<!--golden: @steady/distillation/column14_klemola_c4_splitter kpi splitter Q_reboiler_kW *1e-3-->       9.756<!--golden: kpi splitter Q_reboiler_kW *1e-3-->       10.24
      outer iterations (last pass)          597         711         --

  (Header re-read 2026-11-25 against the engine's own run.  The table had been
  taken before the subcooled reflux was declared and said 94.033 / 93.975 and
  0.0015 / 0.0144 wt %, 0.351 / 0.378 and 97.984 / 97.977, tops at 314.250 /
  314.116 K and 349 / 426 iterations.  The wt % are read from the run's
  distillate and bottoms streams with the catalogue's molar masses, as in
  column14's own section 15, which carries the same table.)

  The efficiency moves the MAJOR components by almost nothing (i-butane
  overhead by 0.07 wt %) and the MINOR key in each product by a real factor
  (n-butane carried overhead rises about 9x when the efficiency is switched
  off; i-butane lost to the bottoms rises 8 %).  At 74 trays and R = 11.4 the
  column is far past the point where separating power limits the majors, so
  extra power goes entirely into the impurity that is left.  column14's
  flowsheetDict section 15 argues that at length; this is the same table so
  that either case can be read alone.

  WHAT THIS ARM ESTABLISHES, and it belongs here because this is the case that
  establishes it: at E_MV = 1 the model already carries 0.0755 wt % n-butane
  overhead against the plant's 0.2, so both arms are CLEANER overhead than the
  plant, and the declared efficiency (0.0082 wt %) moves the model further
  from that one number, not closer.  Recorded; not explained here and not
  tuned away.

  ONE MORE OBSERVATION, and it is about the ENGINE rather than the column.
  This pair used to carry two energy audits that disagreed wildly (column14
  at 98.42 %, this arm at -256.84 %, a residual of 670.7 kW) although the
  product enthalpies agreed to 0.8 kW.  That is gone: both runs now close
  their plant energy boundary with a residual of 0.000 kW.  What remains is a
  CMO property, stated in the run: the reboiler's own envelope on the CMO
  traffic (10.45 MW) does not agree with the plug's duty (9.76 MW), because
  CMO solves no stage energy balance.

  Neither case claims a duty anchor -- see column14's section 8.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

units
(
    {
        name        splitter;
        type        distillationColumn;
        model       WangHenke;                  // as column14 -- see its section 10
        in          feed;
        outputs     ( distillate  bottoms );

        operation
        {
            //  Keys (C504): the paper's two keys, i-butane in the distillate and n-butane in the bottoms (Klemola & Ilme 1996, Table 2)
            lightKey isoButane;  heavyKey nButane;
            nStages        75;                  // 74 real trays + reboiler
            feedStage      37;                  // Table 2, from the top
            P              658.6 kPa;           // Table 2 top pressure

            distillateRate 142.023556 kmol/h;   // 8115 kg/h / 57.13841
            refluxRatio    11.440296;           // 92838 / 8115
            refluxTemperature 18.5 degC;        // Table 2, as column14 (7)
            //  feedQuality 0.9070666555 REMOVED (C235): a derived cross-check of the
            //  engine's own A1 flash, not Klemola's number (the paper gives no feed T).
            //  The stream's resolved state decides; 0/feed T is the old derivation.

            murphreeEfficiency 1.0;             // <- THE ONLY DIFFERENCE
        }

        compositionTol  1e-7;
        maxOuterIter    2000;
    }
);
